{"abstract":"The vaquita is the most critically endangered marine mammal, with fewer than 19 remaining in the wild. First described in 1958, the vaquita has been in rapid decline for more than 20 years resulting from inadvertent deaths due to the increasing use of large-mesh gillnets. To understand the evolutionary and demographic history of the vaquita, we used combined long-read sequencing and long-range scaffolding methods with long- and short-read RNA sequencing to generate a near error-free annotated reference genome assembly from cell lines derived from a female individual. The genome assembly consists of 99.92% of the assembled sequence contained in 21 nearly gapless chromosome-length autosome scaffolds and the X-chromosome scaffold, with a scaffold N50 of 115 Mb. Genome-wide heterozygosity is the lowest (0.01%) of any mammalian species analyzed to date, but heterozygosity is evenly distributed across the chromosomes, consistent with long-term small population size at genetic equilibrium, rather than low diversity resulting from a recent population bottleneck or inbreeding. Historical demography of the vaquita indicates long-term population stability at less than 5000 (Ne) for over 200,000 years. Together, these analyses indicate that the vaquita genome has had ample opportunity to purge highly deleterious alleles and potentially maintain diversity necessary for population health.","author_count":34,"contributors":["Morin, Phillip A","Archer, Frederick I","Avila, Catherine D","Balacco, Jennifer R","Bukhman, Yury V","Chow, William","Fedrigo, Olivier","Formenti, Giulio","Fronczek, Julie A","Fungtammasan, Arkarachai","Gulland, Frances M D","Haase, Bettina","Heide-Jorgensen, Mads Peter","Houck, Marlys L","Howe, Kerstin","Misuraca, Ann C","Mountcastle, Jacquelyn","Musser, Whitney","Paez, Sadye","Pelan, Sarah","Phillippy, Adam","Rhie, Arang","Robinson, Jacqueline","Rojas-Bracho, Lorenzo","Rowles, Teri K","Ryder, Oliver A","Smith, Cynthia R","Stevenson, Sacha","Taylor, Barbara L","Teilmann, Jonas","Torrance, James","Wells, Randall S","Westgate, Andrew J","Jarvis, Erich D"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-261043"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","Europe, Non-EU","Nordic","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Morin, Phillip A"],"country":["Greenland","United Kingdom","United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Greenland Institute of Natural Resources","NOAA","National Human Genome Research Institute","Rockefeller University","University of California at San Diego","University of North Carolina","Wellcome Sanger Institute"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-OT","DI-U"],"level3":{"OT":["Unspecified organisation"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"reference genome and demographic history of the most endangered marine mammal the vaquita","src":["au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1111/1755-0998.13284","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-261043","identifier":[{"id":"PMC8247363","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"33089966","type":"pmid"},{"id":"85096801157","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["au-e6b2a38b-8b02-4878-89e7-4cae2d761d59"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-C4CC127081D18B58DEC64F8409FF3F0F"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.au.dk/ws/files/273771141/Molecular_Ecology_Resources_2020_Morin_Reference_genome_and_demographic_history_of_the_most_endangered_marine_mammal_.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1111/1755-0998.13284","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"