{"abstract":"Post-translational modification of bacterial elongation factor P (EF-P) with (R)-β-lysine at a conserved lysine residue activates the protein in vivo and increases puromycin reactivity of the ribosome in vitro. The additional hydroxylation of EF-P at the same lysine residue by the YfcM protein has also recently been described. The roles of modified and unmodified EF-P during different steps in translation, and how this correlates to its physiological role in the cell, have recently been linked to the synthesis of polyproline stretches in proteins. Polysome analysis indicated that EF-P functions in translation elongation, rather than initiation as proposed previously. This was further supported by the inability of EF-P to enhance the rate of formation of fMet-Lys or fMet-Phe, indicating that the role of EF-P is not to specifically stimulate formation of the first peptide bond. Investigation of hydroxyl-(β)-lysyl-EF-P showed 30% increased puromycin reactivity but no differences in dipeptide synthesis rates when compared with the β-lysylated form. Unlike disruption of the other genes required for EF-P modification, deletion of yfcM had no phenotypic consequences in Salmonella. Taken together, our findings indicate that EF-P functions in translation elongation, a role critically dependent on post-translational β-lysylation but not hydroxylation.","author_count":10,"contributors":["Bullwinkle, Tammy J","Zou, S Betty","Rajkovic, Andrei","Hersch, Steven J","Elgamal, Sara","Robinson, Nathaniel","Smil, David","Bolshan, Yuri","Navarre, William Wiley","Ibba, Michael"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-401987"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Bacterial Proteins","Escherichia coli","Hydroxylation","Lysine","Mixed Function Oxygenases","Peptide Elongation Factors","Protein Processing Post Translational","Salmonella enterica"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"r β lysine modified elongation factor p functions in translation elongation","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1074/jbc.M112.438879","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-401987","identifier":[{"id":"PMC3567691","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"23277358","type":"pmid"},{"id":"84873636344","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["ku-0e6295af-3e70-4a48-8d68-99e243c93b6c"],"keyword":["Bacterial Proteins","Escherichia coli","Hydroxylation","Lysine","Mixed Function Oxygenases","Peptide Chain Elongation, Translational","Peptide Elongation Factors","Protein Processing, Post-Translational","Salmonella enterica"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Local-1-1-89AA641F1EA1AE141DB68B0C0F27D92D"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1074/jbc.M112.438879","version":"Other Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Blocked","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2013","yearSub":"2013"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Cellular and Molecular Medicine, Transcription, RNA, and Gene Medicine Program"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"","first":"Tammy J","key":"bullwinklepau","last":"Bullwinkle","name":"Bullwinkle, Tammy J","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"S Betty","key":"zoupau","last":"Zou","name":"Zou, S Betty","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Andrei","key":"rajkovicpau","last":"Rajkovic","name":"Rajkovic, Andrei","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Steven J","key":"herschpau","last":"Hersch","name":"Hersch, Steven J","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Sara","key":"elgamalpau","last":"Elgamal","name":"Elgamal, Sara","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Nathaniel","key":"robinsonpau","last":"Robinson","name":"Robinson, Nathaniel","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"David","key":"smilpau","last":"Smil","name":"Smil, David","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Yuri","key":"bolshanpau","last":"Bolshan","name":"Bolshan, Yuri","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"William Wiley","key":"navarrepau","last":"Navarre","name":"Navarre, William Wiley","rank":9,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Michael","key":"ibbapau","last":"Ibba","locid":"5995fac4-e31c-44d9-ba6d-fbc1bee09f66","name":"Ibba, Michael","rank":10,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["23277358"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1074/jbc.m112.438879","issn":["00219258","10678816"],"issn_org":["00219258"],"issue":"6","journal":"Journal of Biological Chemistry","pages":"4416-23","status":"p","title":"Journal of Biological Chemistry","title_org":"The Journal of Biological Chemistry","volume":"288"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-0e6295af-3e70-4a48-8d68-99e243c93b6c"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"(R)-β-lysine-modified elongation factor P functions in translation elongation","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-401987","year":"2013","yearsub":"2013"}
{"abstract":"Zebrafish (Danio rerio) are rapidly gaining popularity in translational neuroscience and behavioral research. Physiological similarity to mammals, ease of genetic manipulations, sensitivity to pharmacological and genetic factors, robust behavior, low cost, and potential for high-throughput screening contribute to the growing utility of zebrafish models in this field. Understanding zebrafish behavioral phenotypes provides important insights into neural pathways, physiological biomarkers, and genetic underpinnings of normal and pathological brain function. Novel zebrafish paradigms continue to appear with an encouraging pace, thus necessitating a consistent terminology and improved understanding of the behavioral repertoire. What can zebrafish 'do', and how does their altered brain function translate into behavioral actions? To help address these questions, we have developed a detailed catalog of zebrafish behaviors (Zebrafish Behavior Catalog, ZBC) that covers both larval and adult models. Representing a beginning of creating a more comprehensive ethogram of zebrafish behavior, this effort will improve interpretation of published findings, foster cross-species behavioral modeling, and encourage new groups to apply zebrafish neurobehavioral paradigms in their research. In addition, this glossary creates a framework for developing a zebrafish neurobehavioral ontology, ultimately to become part of a unified animal neurobehavioral ontology, which collectively will contribute to better integration of biological data within and across species.","author_count":29,"contributors":["Kalueff, Allan V","Gebhardt, Michael","Stewart, Adam Michael","Cachat, Jonathan M","Brimmer, Mallorie","Chawla, Jonathan S","Craddock, Cassandra","Kyzar, Evan J","Roth, Andrew","Landsman, Samuel","Gaikwad, Siddharth","Robinson, Kyle","Baatrup, Erik","Tierney, Keith","Shamchuk, Angela","Norton, William","Miller, Noam","Nicolson, Teresa","Braubach, Oliver","Gilman, Charles P","Pittman, Julian","Rosemberg, Denis B","Gerlai, Robert","Echevarria, David","Lamb, Elisabeth","Neuhauss, Stephan C F","Weng, Wei","Bally-Cuif, Laure","Schneider, Henning"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-175159"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","OECD"],"corpau":["Zebrafish Neuroscience Research Consortium"],"country":["United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Tulane University"],"keyword":["Animals","Behavior Animal","Female","Larva","Male","Nervous System Physiological Phenomena","Sex Characteristics","Terminology as Topic","Zebrafish"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"towards a comprehensive catalog of zebrafish behavior 000000000001 000000000000 and beyond","src":["au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1089/zeb.2012.0861","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-175159","identifier":[{"id":"84876267888","type":"scopus"},{"id":"PMC3629777","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"23590400","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["au-505d1be2-fda6-422d-b7dd-6d119acd0b21"],"keyword":["Animals","Behavior, Animal","Female","Larva","Male","Nervous System Physiological Phenomena","Sex Characteristics","Terminology as Topic","Zebrafish"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-31E8F378389301288451C123A014EB19"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1089/zeb.2012.0861","version":"Other Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Unused","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2013","yearSub":"2013"},"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"us","key":"tulane university","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"Tulane University","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"au aarhus unive